# content-seal's starts of its subjects (BL-878 grammar). Every other drive is in process: the arm scripts call the seal's
# functions directly, drive the recorder through acs_main in a subshell, and reach every other subject through
# seal-lib.sh's `bash` shim, which runs a registered subject's entry (merge_main, join_main, vga_main, wfd_mark_main and
# wfd_beat_main, seal_main) in a subshell; a subject that is itself asked in process reaches the seal the same way. The
# programs run only in the cells below, each started FXCELL=<id> command bash and held byte-identical to the in-process
# answer on the same world (seal-lib.sh cs_eq; the wfd cells compare their own pair in merge-arms.sh).
# The merge, one outcome each: the sealed untouched set merges, a sealed shard rewritten through Bash is refused.
cli core/scripts/merge-adversarial-shards.sh cs-merge-merged
cli core/scripts/merge-adversarial-shards.sh cs-merge-refused
# The join: sealed untouched parts join, a python3-rewritten sealed part is refused.
cli core/scripts/join-remediator-shards.sh cs-join-joined
cli core/scripts/join-remediator-shards.sh cs-join-refused
# The gate validator: --expected, and --merge's two outcomes.
cli core/scripts/validate-gate-adjudication.sh cs-gate-expected
cli core/scripts/validate-gate-adjudication.sh cs-gate-pass
cli core/scripts/validate-gate-adjudication.sh cs-gate-refuse
# wait-for-deliverable.sh: --mark refused and passing, --status on a CURRENT and a LEGACY role world.
cli core/scripts/wait-for-deliverable.sh cs-wfd-mark-refuse
cli core/scripts/wait-for-deliverable.sh cs-wfd-mark-pass
cli core/scripts/wait-for-deliverable.sh cs-wfd-status-current
cli core/scripts/wait-for-deliverable.sh cs-wfd-status-legacy
# gate_adjudication.py, which gate-unit.py runs for every in-process --merge decision, starts the shipped seal as a program (subprocess) for
# --agent-ere, --check, --row and --first-tool-ts; python cannot call a shell function, so those starts cannot be in process.
e2e core/scripts/content-seal.sh cs-gate-seal-1 why=gate_adjudication.py starts the shipped seal with subprocess.run on every in-process --merge decision, and python cannot call a shell function
e2e core/scripts/content-seal.sh cs-gate-seal-2 why=gate_adjudication.py starts the shipped seal with subprocess.run on every in-process --merge decision, and python cannot call a shell function
